A||||29||Roy||Audio Question
Z||000000||Roy||05-10-99||07:02 PM||||Im considering putting in a custom audio system, do to the car right now only has 2 4x10's. And some cheesy radio shack cassette deck. I was wondering what have some of you done to your fiero's and how does it sound? 
Z||000001||EMF||05-10-99||07:47 PM||||every radio/speaker combo is  loud in the fiero. <br>hehe
Z||000002||Patrick||05-10-99||08:03 PM||||<br>When I bought my '87 Fiero, it came with speakers in the dash, and speakers in the sail panels, AND speakers in the headrests of the seats. (The seats must have been from an older model.) The sound system still sounded crummy though. Anyway, I put in a radio/cassette deck that had a built in <b>5-band graphic equalizer</b> and that has made a world of difference.<p>Because the space you are trying to fill with sound is relatively small, you don't need a whole lot of power to pump the speakers. As EMF says, it's going to be loud.<p><p><p>
Z||000003||sluppy123||05-10-99||08:05 PM||||I have a fairly nice system in my 87' GT. It consists of a pioneer tape deck with a 12 disc cd changer mounted in the trunk. 4 x 10 pioneer 3-ways in the dash, pioneer 4 x 6 in the rear. I custom made a kick panel to hold a 8 in rockford fosgate sub on the passenger side. I wanted more bass so i made a sealed box to hold a 12 in sub up front and moved the spare tire and jack to the trunk.<p>To power all of this I placed two Rockfod fosgate Punch 200 4a amps behind each seat. <br> <br>It turns some heads and makes people look twice when they see where all that noise is comming from. Then they ask what it is and I say... THAT'S A FIERO!!!
Z||000004||theogre||05-10-99||09:56 PM||||I'll assume we are talking about good sound, and not making a boom box on wheels. Nearly any retard can make loud noise.... Good sound is an art form.<p>I run the OEM radio ((Delco has always made excelent sounding products even if the tape drive mechanicals are a bit week)) and some after market speakers. When none of the 3 speakers is more than 3 feet away from your ear,,,,, you won't ever want to sit there at more than 1/2 volume for long. (after so many years the speakers are just shot.... the entire suround on my front speakers had rotted away. UV, Ozone, etc just hate foam surounds. the back ones are marginal and will be replaced soon.)<p>Add in even a small sub woofer........ and all you need is a small one...... and you'll have very nice sound in the car.<p>BTW, there are mounting kits and wiring harness adaptors for GM cars. Use them. These kits will mount many kinds of after market sound systems and will not make you do any cutting to the factory wire. Any additional wire for sub's, etc, can be inserted between the kit and the new radio.
Z||000005||Lynx||05-11-99||12:52 AM||||If you want to know about car stereo im the guy to ask. Like the others said you dont need to get big the car is small. First just buy a good cd player and mount it in with a kit or by a pioneer that are made to fit in GM cars. Ok Pioneer also makes some very good 4x10's but if you want to go better you can fit componets sets in the dash(up to 5.25) this all requires some knowledge on how to make custom plates. Most of the cars had the dumb head rest speakers (my 85 sports coupe does) Dont Use Them. If you want you could get sail panels from a newer car then you could have 4x6's behind you. You always have the option of a amp but a high power deck is plenty unless you want a sub. With the sub situation you ethere have to be short or have small subs behind the seat, like no bigger than 8's(there are 6.5's subs too). There is also room under the dash for a small sub most likey a 6.5 i doubt if you could fit a 8 in there(passenger side). Im going to the stereo store tommorow to check what the can fit. I hope that gives you some options.
Z||000006||PaUL||05-11-99||02:14 PM||||Forget audio CDs and CD changers, the future of recorded audio is the MP3 format. What I want is a MP3 player for a car. That with digital input speakers so you would get no noise whatsoever. Ive seen some custom built mp3 units on the net. These were made from old CD head units but modified to play mp3 files burned onto a CD. In mp3 format a CD can hold up to 200 4 min. songs, so you would need only a few CDs. Ive listened to lots of mp3s on my PC and to me they sound better than the original CD. <p>Ive been thinking I could build an entire PC for my car just to play music, using the guts from a laptop.   
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||05-11-99||03:24 PM||||There's already a consumer product that plays MP3 (Rio). It's like a small walkman and you could plug it into your Aux In of your CD/Radio (if it has one). It holds something like 40-50 minutes of music which is uploaded to the Rio using your computer.
Z||000008||PaUL||05-11-99||03:52 PM||||yeah you could do that but it wouldnt look too good. What I want is something that has everything in one unit.
Z||000009||overlord||05-12-99||05:31 PM||||ya..mp3s are the future...(im listneing now), they sound great..in some cases better than cd quality.<p>its just a matter of time before they start making mp3 players for car audio (minidisks are here now so.....eventually)<p>Best right now is just to burn mp3s onto a cd...it can hold so mych more music than cd auido files.
