Z||000020||Racer 1||06-10-99||12:19 PM||||One way to install bigger speakers under the front dash is to use plexiglass.first cut in the middle of the plexyglass a 4"by8" opening and drill out the mounting holes for the factory 4-10 in your dash then cut out the outer diameter of the bigger speaker and the mounting holes and mount it under the dash..Ive seen a fiero who made 10" in the fire wall behind the seats he said there is a dead space behind there about 4 or so inches.
Z||000021||batboy||06-13-99||01:45 PM||||Some great ideas in this discussion, not to mention some mega expensive stereo systems. I'm going to try TooLowFiero's idea about mounting 6x9 speakers in the dash in my lastest V8 Fiero. Lynx seems to know his stuff too. My other Fiero is a daily driver and this is what I did to get a decent sounding system on a LOW budget. I'm over 40 years old, so I don't have the need to impress people two blocks away with a loud stereo, but I love good quality sound.<p>Use a inexpensive (not the cheapest) name brand CD player (I've had great luck with my Sony) and matched set of good quality name brand speakers (I used 4x10 Kicker Impulse in the front and 4x6 ones in the rear sail panels). I first tried cheaper Jensens but blew them out in no time. The Kickers sound wonderful and are reliable. This set-up is 500 times better than the factory rig. If you crave more bass (I'm not talking about monster ghetto thump here), use an efficient 8 inch subwoofer that works well in a very small (0.25 to 0.36 cu. ft.) closed box. The Polk db, JBL GT, or other good name brand sub will work. Then build a custom box using good quality materials to fit behind the pasenger seat. Finish it off with a good name brand amp behind the driver seat (about 75 to 150 watts bridged to the sub) will provide plenty of bass punch in a small Fiero cockpit. There will be people out there that will disagree with me, but on a low budget, this sounds awesome. Obviously, if you have deep pockets and want a system that is more expensive than your Fiero, or one that will blow your windows out, then go for it. It's your money.
Z||000022||Racer 1||06-13-99||08:55 PM||||Ive always get some of my stuff at my local pawn shop.I have done over 5000.00$ worth of business with him so he gives me real good deals on amps and speakers,etc..I got a real good deal on a kenwood 501 cassette deck with a 10 disk cd changer for only 200.00$ and all my stuff is guaranteed to work or my money back!!You dont have to buy brand new just make sure the stuff you get is in working condition.MR.BIG BASS HEAD   ....PEACE....
Z||000023||mwbackus||06-14-99||02:15 AM||||I really love a nice stereo system, but about 2 years ago I was blessed with this nice thing called tinitus due to upper hearing loss. ( I am 43 ).  Too many rock concerts and loud stereos caused this loss of hearing and the resulting tinitus.  For a little hint of what tinitus is like, try to recall one of those star trek episodes where the aliens attack the crew with those high pitched weapons.  They immediatly grab their ears, fall to the floor, and pass out.  Mine is constant. 24hrs, every day. But, I usually dont pass out...  Just something to think about next time you crank the volume. :-)
Z||000024||lowCG||06-14-99||01:51 PM||||I spent a few years working in a machine shop,and fear someday this may happen,hope not.I enjoy sound in my house that many of my friends can't distinguish from stereos that I think sound horrible.I would feel a great loss without this.<br>It's real easy to exceed safe levels in a car while attempting to drown out road noise with cheap tweeters and no crossovers.
Z||000025||Racer 1||06-14-99||02:10 PM||||I dont crank up the volume everytime I drive my fiero.Maybe about once a month around nightfall the the city strip starts coming alive with HOT women and nice cars.Then we all meet at a parking lot and just have a good time.Ive been in some suv's and low rider trucks with BIG speakers 15"-32" now thats loud.But to me I like to hear everything highs,mids,and lows not just booooooom boooooooom and no voices.Just cause I like BIG BASS doesnt mean that I do it everyday!!besides my 8" bazookas dont come close to the spl level the suv's do!!CRANK IT UP &lt;-------- PEACE ---
Z||000026||mwbackus||06-14-99||06:30 PM||||I still enjoy a good system, and occasionally even a bit on the loud side.  The damage that I have experienced comes not from a being subjected to a constant background noise, but was commulative over years of occasional (~ 1 per mo) 2 - 3 hr exposures to very high Db's.  The rock concerts I enjoyed when I was younger, dwarf the sound systems found in all but the highest end car audio systems.  I am concerned with this trend over the past 5 to 7 yrs towards these more powerful systems (over 110 Db's).  This approaches and even surpases the levels in which damage occurs.  If your ears ring after exposure to these levels, you know that some damage has been done. No one ever informed me that these episodes could actually cause this degree of damage. (I probably would not have listened even if they did).  I always thought that hearing loss was no big deal, you just have to pay more attention or get a hearing aid. Tinitus, although not a rare condition by no means, does not happen with all incidence of hearing loss, but when it does occur it severly impacts the quality of your life. I just hate to think what may happen to "some" of these kids 20 to 30 yrs from now.
Z||000027||batboy||06-15-99||08:01 PM||||The possible hearing loss due to listening to excessively loud stereos is a real concern. However, when it comes to the younger generation, your "sound" advice is undoubtedly falling on "deaf" ears. Pun intended (lol).
Z||000028||grizco||06-17-99||03:56 PM||||For in car mp3 player info check out this link. http://www.ryanspc.com/carmp3/mp3player.html He built the system for less than $400 and has a list of all the parts needed.
Z||000029||PaUL||06-17-99||05:23 PM||||ok grizco I checked it out. This is what I dont want, something that looks cobbled together and shodily constructed. <p>On the topic of mp3 sound quality compared to audio CDs, I agree that the audio cd has way more data than the compressed mp3 format but the hardware used to convert the data into audioable sound is the difference. Most consumer audio CD players dont play the entire spectrum of sound recorded on the CD because of inferior hardware. The sound quality of mp3s are dependant on software and the hardware used to play them and since most sound cards can produce sound frequencies beyond what any audio CD player can do, they can sound better. Thats why I think when I play a song on my PC from an audio CD then listen to the same song in mp3, the mp3 sounds better. The CD drive doesnt play audio CDs well at all. It would sound better if I had a sound card with a digital audio input hooked up to the digital out on the CD drive. 
